The Cutting Machine
[trigger none]
[vignette dungeons/foundry]

FIXME(action): The shriek grows until it is the only thing in the world: one machine taller than a house, working in the dark. A blade drives down through stacked metal plate, cutting each one into a shape for some later assembly. Where the edge bites, the metal flares with the Color and then cools to a low glow, the way a worked piece holds heat after the tool has passed.
FIXME(dread): Suhail is talking. Over the noise you cannot make out a word. He walks out of the lamplight toward the machine. It thunks, drops in pitch, grinds down to silence. You find him again by his voice, off in the dark to one side, already mid-sentence.

choices:

* Hear out the rest = Let him finish at the machine
    FIXME(dread): He works the shutdown, unlatching a housing along the cutting head, and draws the blade out of it: the length of an arm, the edge still faintly lit. He wraps the tang in a rag. Where a man dressed like he sleeps in ditches learned this machine, he does not say.
    FIXME(revelation): He holds the blade out with some admiration. He was there, he says, when this all started, nearly two hundred years ago. He says it as though this were unremarkable. He says his clan had heard of an order of monks guarding a treasure. The monks fought. He pantomimes it with the blade as he tells how he fought his way through the monastery.
    FIXME(revelation): What they found was a seal tall as a man, cut into the living stone, inlaid with gold and silver, some demon or guardian deity. Not the treasure they wanted. They pried the metal loose; it would sell. Then a blinding flash, the first time anyone had seen the Color, then sickness and an afterglow, then nothing.
    FIXME(dread): Those who had been there developed a mania for order over the following weeks. It took hold of the whole clan: war plans grew meticulous, conquered villages were absorbed into administration rather than burned, supply lines made certain. What they first called the "ordering sickness" they later called the Lattice, the ambient order that aligned the growing empire.
    FIXME(action): Then blade is at your throat. "I could open you here. Flick of the wrist." He is looking past you, at a monk centuries dead, and for a moment does not seem to know which of you is in front of him. Then it passes. He makes a short tired sound that is not a laugh and presses the blade into your hands. "Take it. You'll rest easier with me unarmed."
